{"from": "text:pliny-nh-12-nard", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-14-03-05-nard", "grade": "C", "note": "Pliny prices pure nard at 100 denarii a pound, with graded leaf preparations at 40-75 denarii - nard was a known, costly luxury perfume oil in the Roman world, as Mark's 'pure nard - very costly' and its 300-denarii valuation presuppose. He does not price a single flask of the quality described."}
{"from": "artifact:denarius", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-14-03-05-nard", "grade": "C", "note": "The denarius was the standard Roman silver coin of the 1st century CE - the unit in which Mark prices the ointment ('more than three hundred denarii', cf. Mark 6:37's 200 denarii of bread). Attests the currency of the valuation, not the price itself."}
{"from": "text:pliny-nh-12-nard", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-pliny-nh-12"}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-5-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-14-12-sacrificed-passover", "grade": "B", "note": "Pesachim 5:1 fixes the Temple's festival schedule: 'On the eves of Passover, when the Paschal lamb must be offered after the daily offering', the daily offering is slaughtered and sacrificed earlier so the Passover lambs can be offered that afternoon - the same Jerusalem institution and day Mark names ('when they sacrificed the Passover'). Compiled c. 200 CE and may not describe 30 CE practice exactly."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-5-1", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:sefaria-pesachim-5-1-lamb"}
{"from": "artifact:tyrian-shekel", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-14-11-money-promised", "grade": "C", "note": "The Tyrian shekel (126 BC-AD 56) was the silver coin of Judea's economy and the Temple; payment 'to give him money' for a deliverance-betrayal fits the same silver economy that weighed out Matthew's thirty pieces. It attests the coinage of such a payment, not the promise itself."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-bava-kamma-7-7", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:mk-14-30-cockcrow-twice", "note": "Tension: Bava Kamma 7:7 rules 'One may not raise chickens in Jerusalem, due to the sacrificial meat that is common there' - if the rule held in 30 CE, the second cockcrow of 14:72 would not normally sound inside the city where the courtyard scene sits (it could come from houses outside the walls; the Mishnah is c. 200 CE and may not describe 30 CE practice exactly)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-bava-kamma-7-7", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:sefaria-bava-kamma-7-7"}
{"from": "site:cenacle", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-14-15-upper-room", "grade": "D", "note": "The Cenacle on Mount Zion is 'traditionally held to be the site of the Last Supper' - a reception tradition, and the standing Gothic room is dated to the 12th-14th centuries, well over a millennium after 30 CE. Reception: it attests the tradition, not the room of Mark 14:15."}
{"from": "site:cenacle", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-cenacle"}
{"from": "claim:mk-14-13-water-carrier", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-water-carrier"}
{"from": "claim:mk-14-36-abba", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-abba-aramaic"}
{"from": "claim:mk-14-51-young-man-fled", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-naked-fugitive"}
{"from": "claim:mk-14-11-money-promised", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-26-15-thirty-silver", "note": "Same bargain, different detail: Mark says the chief priests 'promised to give him money' with no amount; Matthew 26:15 specifies thirty pieces of silver weighed out. A difference between Gospels, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:mk-14-30-cockcrow-twice", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-26-34-cockcrow-denial", "note": "Same prediction, different detail: Mark 14:30 (and 14:72) says 'before the rooster crows TWICE'; Matthew 26:34 has a single cockcrow and no 'twice'. A difference between Gospels, not evidence; the variant history is on passage:mk-14-30."}
{"from": "claim:mk-14-27-strike-shepherd-quote", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:zechariah-13-7", "note": "Biblical cross-reference: Mark 14:27 quotes Zechariah 13:7 ('strike the shepherd... the sheep will be scattered') with 'it is written', adding 'I' and dropping the address 'O sword'."}
{"from": "claim:mk-14-58-destroy-temple-saying", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-24-01-02-temple-thrown-down", "note": "The reported saying of 14:58 ('I will destroy this temple... in three days I will build another') presupposes the same Temple and its fate as the 24:2 prediction, but it is a hostile report at a hearing, not the discourse prediction; related, not the same claim."}
